#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064976} :activity/id #uuid "0daebd08-5b3a-5348-97e2-30af6666d226" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050865 :topic/id #uuid "6c838d5e-1457-5ed8-957b-544017d2ddfe" :topic/math-academy-id 341} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065391 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below.\n![](images/20/20b2fd99ae051c3447e4c3530fee053b248393147a8295d3a9451524d7534f52.svg)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=4.$ However, by selecting a particular value for $a,$ we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $a$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x) = f(4)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $4.$ So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x),$ we need to use the function definition for $x \\neq 4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}(3x+a) \\\\  & =3(4)+a \\\\  & =12+a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=4,$ the function is defined as $f(x)=13.$ So $f(4)=13,$ and we can solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & =f(4) \\\\ 12+a & =13 \\\\ a & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=4,$ we need to choose $a=1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ec3c5bfd-8781-5ecb-9e08-fe301193f6ea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 535 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "14b7beb0-d51f-5caa-8ccf-0fcaf66bc2a2" :step/math-academy-id 1003 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065161}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064977 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064150 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064033 :question/id #uuid "e6c4219b-1955-5fa0-a362-6cc273510d88" :question/math-academy-id "